Three places in the C rely on implementation-defined or wrapping behaviour, and I reproduced rather than fixed them:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- In [38;5;10mstbds_siphash_bytes[0m, [38;5;10md[0] | (d[1]<<8) | (d[2]<<16) | (d[3]<<24)[0m is computed in [38;5;10mint[0m, so a high bit in [38;5;10md[3][0m yields a negative [38;5;10mint[0m that [1msign-extends[22m when assigned to [38;5;10msize_t[0m, setting the whole upper 32 bits. Same for [38;5;10mcase 4:[0m in the tail switch. Translated as [38;5;10mi32[0m arithmetic followed by [38;5;10mas isize as usize[0m.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mhash ^= hash ^ ROTR(hash,n)[0m is a no-op XOR that reduces to a plain rotate; kept written literally.[0m[0m
- The [38;5;10mstbds_stralloc[0m oversized-block path links the new block in [3mafter[23m [38;5;10ma->storage[0m rather than at the head and leaves [38;5;10mremaining[0m untouched — preserved as-is.[0m[0m
